1
00:00:00,570 --> 00:00:08,800
Let's move now to access methods. So how to
access files and which is the minimum unit

2
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:16,420
we can read or write from secondary storage
or the disk so we can go in two ways here one is

3
00:00:16,420 --> 00:00:18,039
sequential access.

4
00:00:18,039 --> 00:00:19,240
So the idea is that

5
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:24,580
we start reading a file from the very beginning
and then we have functionality that allows

6
00:00:24,580 --> 00:00:26,769
us to

7
00:00:26,769 --> 00:00:30,099
take the next

8
00:00:30,099 --> 00:00:36,170
let's say piece of information in the file,
we will see this is called block, or to go

9
00:00:36,170 --> 00:00:42,390
back. So it's like scanning a file. This could
be a way to access the information in a file

10
00:00:42,390 --> 00:00:46,390
and the operating system can give you

11
00:00:46,390 --> 00:00:48,430
functionality to do this sequential read.

12
00:00:48,430 --> 00:00:55,840
But we can also have direct access, so we
might want functionality that allows us to

13
00:00:55,840 --> 00:00:59,539
read or write at specific offsets in the file, so that

14
00:00:59,539 --> 00:01:04,320
we can rapidly go where we want instead of
just scanning everything it depends on the

15
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:05,320
type of file. If you have to

16
00:01:05,320 --> 00:01:11,140
listen to a song that is in a file, then most
likely you are going to scan everything because

17
00:01:11,140 --> 00:01:17,170
you have to read the entire file in order.
If you are accessing a data file and you only

18
00:01:17,170 --> 00:01:21,360
need to take some specific data values and
you know how the file is organized like a

19
00:01:21,360 --> 00:01:26,500
matrix, maybe then you can go in a specific
offset and read the stuff.

20
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:33,170
Now, independently of which one of the two
we use,

21
00:01:33,170 --> 00:01:38,860
the operating system reasons in terms of blocks.

22
00:01:38,860 --> 00:01:44,490
So whether we read one thing after the other or
we access something at a specific position,

23
00:01:44,490 --> 00:01:51,479
the minimum unit we can use to interact with
the disk is called block, which is a group

24
00:01:51,479 --> 00:01:52,800
of or

25
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:59,479
a certain amount of bytes, as you can imagine. So why
do we read blocks instead of just bites? Why

26
00:01:59,479 --> 00:02:04,910
don't we go "give me the next byte" or "give
me / go to a specific byte"? Remember

27
00:02:04,910 --> 00:02:09,330
this is not inside the memory. This is when
we actually want to read something from the

28
00:02:09,330 --> 00:02:10,330
disk.

29
00:02:10,330 --> 00:02:17,500
Well, you remember this. We saw some lectures
ago that whenever you have to read or write

30
00:02:17,500 --> 00:02:24,860
something from the disk, the CPU cannot directly
communicate with the disk, it needs to go through

31
00:02:24,860 --> 00:02:31,630
several cycles because as we said, the CPU
only reaches main memory directly, which means

32
00:02:31,630 --> 00:02:36,450
we will have system calls, context switches,
we have to move processes in a waiting

33
00:02:36,450 --> 00:02:37,450
queue

34
00:02:37,450 --> 00:02:42,100
we need to wait for the input output of the
disk, interrupts and so on, so you can imagine

35
00:02:42,100 --> 00:02:47,300
that pay all this price every time you need
a byte and you have to read a very large file it

36
00:02:47,300 --> 00:02:51,800
doesn't really make sense, right? it's
too much overhead.

37
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:57,760
So instead what we do is that if we read or
write something, then we take a chunk of the

38
00:02:57,760 --> 00:03:05,050
file, because similar in spirit to the concept
of locality. You know that if you need a certain

39
00:03:05,050 --> 00:03:12,370
byte most likely you also need bites that are
around that one.

40
00:03:12,370 --> 00:03:14,630
So now the question is:

41
00:03:14,630 --> 00:03:21,120
fine, let's use blocks instead of bytes, how
big should a block be?

42
00:03:21,120 --> 00:03:25,840
If the minimum unit, the block, is all you
can read or write and you choose a very large

43
00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:31,560
block. The good thing is that you don't need
to read or write a lot of blocks when you

44
00:03:31,560 --> 00:03:36,950
have to store or read something, on the other
end, if a block is let's say 1 megabyte and

45
00:03:36,950 --> 00:03:39,990
you want only to write a file that takes a
couple of bites,

46
00:03:39,990 --> 00:03:47,370
then you're going to waste a big chunk
of that block because you can only write blocks.

47
00:03:47,370 --> 00:03:53,430
On the other hand, if you use small blocks,
you can better utilize the disk because you

48
00:03:53,430 --> 00:03:58,810
waste less space, but then you need to read
or write or access in general more blocks

49
00:03:58,810 --> 00:04:02,180
when you have to do something with a file.

50
00:04:02,180 --> 00:04:09,590
And actually, you can see that, as always,
this tradeoffs

51
00:04:09,590 --> 00:04:17,690
result in different behaviors. So in this
graph here you have different block sizes

52
00:04:17,690 --> 00:04:21,790
from one kilobyte to 1 megabyte, and then
you have to lines. The solid one is the disk

53
00:04:21,790 --> 00:04:22,790
space.

54
00:04:22,790 --> 00:04:29,241
and the dashed one is the data rate. So what are we seeing
here? If you look at the data rate, which

55
00:04:29,241 --> 00:04:30,710
is the

56
00:04:30,710 --> 00:04:34,050
dashed one you see that

57
00:04:34,050 --> 00:04:37,540
the larger the block

58
00:04:37,540 --> 00:04:42,460
the higher the data rate.

59
00:04:42,460 --> 00:04:50,770
So basically you can go up to 60 megabytes
per second if you have blocks of 1 megabyte.

60
00:04:50,770 --> 00:04:56,700
Depends of course on what disk you use its own
speed and so on, but the idea is that this

61
00:04:56,700 --> 00:05:02,020
will grow, but on the other hand, if you have
small blocks, and need to read a lot of them,

62
00:05:02,020 --> 00:05:06,841
then the data rate will decrease because you
have to do more context switches and so, on

63
00:05:06,841 --> 00:05:10,560
the other hand, if you look at the disk space,
which is the solid one, you see that if you

64
00:05:10,560 --> 00:05:12,000
have very small blocks

65
00:05:12,000 --> 00:05:17,030
which means you don't waste blocks when you
have to write small amounts of bytes,

66
00:05:17,030 --> 00:05:23,470
you can use almost the entire disk to
actually store the information you want, but

67
00:05:23,470 --> 00:05:29,100
if you start having large blocks,
then there are good chances that you will

68
00:05:29,100 --> 00:05:35,340
waste space in these blocks and the total
disk utilization will go rapidly through towards

69
00:05:35,340 --> 00:05:36,390
the 0%.

70
00:05:36,390 --> 00:05:42,770
Don't get confused here. You are utilizing the disk at 100%, if you

71
00:05:42,770 --> 00:05:48,730
write all the blocks but you are not really
utilizing 100% of the disk because many of these

72
00:05:48,730 --> 00:05:55,680
blocks do not really contain or their content
is not completely related to what you're writing

73
00:05:55,680 --> 00:06:01,211
is just wasted space because you have to write
a MB despite the fact that you actually just

74
00:06:01,211 --> 00:06:05,440
need a couple of bites for that.

75
00:06:05,440 --> 00:06:10,830
So tradeoffs as always and one of the complicated
things here, we already mentioned this also

76
00:06:10,830 --> 00:06:14,860
for memories that if you know

77
00:06:14,860 --> 00:06:19,270
what is the average size of your file, then
you can probably take the best decision to

78
00:06:19,270 --> 00:06:24,650
maximize space utilization while having still
a good data rate, but you cannot really take

79
00:06:24,650 --> 00:06:28,460
such a decision in advance because when you
design the file system you don't know yet

80
00:06:28,460 --> 00:06:31,530
what files will be stored. That depends on
the user and how he uses the computer, and

81
00:06:31,530 --> 00:06:37,000
so on and so forth, which of course complicates
even more the

82
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:38,080
matter here.

83
00:06:38,080 --> 00:06:44,740
This is just a question I just answered.
So why is it challenging to choose the right

84
00:06:44,740 --> 00:06:50,030
block size? Because you don't know in advance
what the user will actually store on the disk.

